urb drops both qualifications. This is not fabricated, but it is presented too strongly for a third-party anecdotal claim. See [building.json line 52](</Users/rodneylewis/Sprintz/Internal/rl22.github.io/.claude/worktrees/unhide-homepage-sections-b06d72/app/data/building.json:52>) and the [current Superpowers README](https://github.com/obra/superpowers#how-it-works).\n\n5. **Medium \u2014 discipline values are correct, but the metadata contract is not.**\n\n> `\"category\": [\"AI\", \"ENGINEERING\", \"PRODUCT\"]`\n\nThose are the required tags in the required order, and the body supports all three. However, guidance section 1 says to store disciplines as an array without replacing the existing editorial `category`. The implementation stores disciplines in `category` and has no separate discipline field. `type: \"repo review\"` partly preserves the format elsewhere, but it still does not follow the stated data contract. See [building.json line 46](</Users/rodneylewis/Sprintz/Internal/rl22.github.io/.claude/worktrees/unhide-homepage-sections-b06d72/app/data/building.json:46>).\n\n6. **Medium \u2014 this sentence is rhetorically neat but factually slippery.**\n\n> \u201cNeither repository asks me to trust a better prompt.\u201d\n\nBoth repositories primarily package Markdown instructions that become model context\u2014effectively prompts. Their advantage is that those instructions are inspectable, version-controlled, composable, and sometimes backed by scripts and review machinery. Claiming they are not \u201cbetter prompts\u201d creates a false distinction.\n\n7. **Medium \u2014 the ending repeats the preceding section instead of advancing it.**\n\n> \u201cI would use Pocock\u2019s skills for day-to-day work in an established codebase.\u201d\n\nThen eight lines later:\n\n> \u201cFor my day-to-day work in an established codebase, I would pick Pocock\u2019s skills.\u201d\n\nThe same duplication happens with the Superpowers recommendation. The required \u201cIf I could on